  interestingFactsEn: "The life and scientific legacy of Leonardo of Pizansky, who we know as Fibonacci, is full of amazing coincidences, funny historical curryries and facts, which you can't call interesting.\n\nThe mystery of the name: which did not really exist. One of the most amazing facts is that in the life of a scientist no one called him \"Phibonacci.\" This nickname, by which he is known to the world, came more than 600 years after his death, in 1838, thanks to historian Guillaume Libri. Where did the name come from? He was the son of a merchant, Guillelo Bonacci.In time, this word has been reduced and transformed into a compact Fibonacci. What did he call himself? In his writings Leonardo signed as Leonardo Pizano (Leonardo Pisano, pointing to the place of birth) or used the nickname Bigolo.Which is true, and what is a modern - day mockery of useless science remains a mystery.\n\nThe most famous and \"uneffective\" task The sequence that obliterated his name came from a task that was... very far from the reality of medieval life. In his \"Liber Abaci\" book, he put a couple of rabbits in the race and asked how many pairs would be born in a year if each couple gave a new pair every month, and the rabbits certainly never died.The sequence that came out was 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13... where each number, the sum of the two previous ones, became much more famous than the Book of Abaka itself.\n\n♪ Nature, art, and money: the magic of numbers everywhere The Fibonacci sequence was not just a mathematical game, its patterns, particularly its connection to the gold section (number Fi, ; 1.618), appear in the most unexpected places.The numbers of Fibonacci can be seen in the number of petals in flowers, in the spirals of pine pineapples and pineapples, in the location of seeds in the sunflower and even in the structure of the Nautilus shell. In art and music: The ideal proportions of the gold section were widely believed to have been used by artists and architects, from Leonardo da Vinci to El Salvador Dali, and composers like Debussy and Bartok found inspiration in rhythms built on the numbers of Fibonacci.In the world of finance: The sequence has been applied even on the exchange. There are levels of correction of Fibonacci (e.g. 23.6 per cent, 38.2 per cent, 61.8 per cent) that traders use to predict possible price reversals.\n\nThe master event at the court of Emperor Slav of the scientist attracted the attention of the emperor of the Holy Roman Empire of Friedrich II. Around 1225, a real mathematical tournament was held at the court, where the court philosopher John of Palermo had three major tasks. One of them was to solve the cubic equation of x3 + 2x2 + 10x = 20.Fibonacci not only solved it, but also found the root with phenomenal accuracy to the ninth decimal place (1,368808107...), leaving no record of his calculation method, which was forever ahead of its time.\n\nThe book of Abaka is more than just a textbook, although we remember it in sequence, its main contribution to science is the book of Abaka itself. This book was the first major work in Europe, which was: 1. It introduced the Indo-Arab numerals (0-9) and explained the principle of their positional recording. 2. It offered a more convenient recording of fractions (inducted a fraction) and a quadratic root designation.In fact, it became an encyclopedia of mathematical knowledge for medieval Europe, where many generations of students studied.\n\nThe last \"thank you\" from the hometown is the last documentary evidence of Fibonacci's life dated from 1240, and the Republic of Pisa issued an annual pension order of 20 lire, which referred to him as \"the serious and academic master of Leonardo Bigolo\", and which was a tribute to the city for its long-standing work for the benefit of citizens, particularly in the field of accounting and commercial settlements.I hope that these facts have allowed a fresh look at Fibonacci's identity, a man whose name became a legend after centuries."
